## The Numbers: A Recap of the Recall Landscape.
This year alone, there have been 509 food recalls, with 152 classified as Class I, indicating a high risk of severe health consequences. With 83 repeat offenders in play, it's alarming to note that many of the same brands appear time and again. This past week has added to the tally, with nine new recalls, all within the Class I or II categories. Is it a systemic oversight or just plain negligence? You decide.

## Notable Mentions: Who Made the List This Week
Taking center stage is Legacy Bakehouse LLC, with an assortment of bagel crisps and pita chips recalling due to contamination with Salmonella-laden milk powder from California Dairies. Alongside them, Wawa Beverage Company had several of its pint-sized beverages recalled due to undeclared milk allergens. For those with nut allergies, Fly By Jing's Creamy Sesame Noodles might be a concern due to potential peanut cross-contamination. Finally, Ghirardelli Chocolate Company has had its Sweet Ground Powder White Chocolate recalled for potential Salmonella contamination.

## Brands to Watch: Repeat Offenders
Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC top the charts with the highest number of Class I recalls. Ghirardelli Chocolate Company and Everpress Juice are not far behind. Consumers should exercise a heightened degree of caution when purchasing products from these brands.

## What's Behind the Numbers?
It's worth noting that allergens and Salmonella remain the most common causes of recalls. Mislabeling, while not as frequent, still poses considerable risks, especially for individuals with food allergies. As consumers, it's imperative to stay informed and check recall lists regularly to avoid potential health hazards.
